%U A401828 188,194,228,244,220,212,200,204,224,218,248,264,270,268,276,280,284,268,254,264,272,260,304
%N A401828 Number of firing cells after n generations of a four-state cellular automaton on the infinite square lattice (rule B2/S34/C4, see Comments), starting with two horizontally adjacent firing cells and all other cells ready.
%C A401828 The states are 0 (ready), 1 (firing), 2 and 3. Updates are synchronous and count firing cells among the eight cells horizontally, vertically or diagonally adjacent to a cell (its Moore neighbors). A ready cell becomes 1 if the count is 2, and stays 0 otherwise. A firing cell stays firing iff the count is 3 or 4; otherwise it becomes 2. States 2 and 3 become 3 and 0, respectively. Initially only (0,0) and (1,0) fire.
%C A401828 In the code B2/S34/C4, B2 (birth) means that a ready cell fires when the count is 2, S34 (survival) that a firing cell keeps firing when the count is 3 or 4, and C4 that there are 4 states. Rules given by such a code are called "Generations" rules; the program MCell writes the code as survival/birth/states, here 34/2/4 (see the Wójtowicz link).
%C A401828 Every term is even: reflection about x = 1/2 pairs the firing cells and has no fixed lattice cell.
%C A401828 It appears that the first differences A401829, colored by n mod 4, form four crossing bands for 0 <= n < 20000; see the plot.
%C A401828 The leading front is a perpetual rake factory: in coordinates z = y - n, the complete region z >= -520 is periodic with least period 192 from n = 1967 on, and since cells behind it cannot influence it, this holds forever. In each period it creates a new inward rake (a moving emitter), complete at n = 1974 + 192*k, k >= 0; the reflected front does the same.
%C A401828 Exact replays verify a two-generation family tree of rakes: pulses emitted by a rake at n = 852..856 help create a new rake at n = 1014, and a pulse that rake emits at n = 1066 helps create another rake at n = 1188. Proofs and details are in the Additional comments.
